{"tag":"NQ92","body":"<div class=\"pq-q ue-math\"><p>Consider a monopolist with cost function <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"c(q)=cq.\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">q</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">q</span></span><span class=\"mord\">.</span></span></span></span></span> Use a simple revealed preference argument to show that the monopolist’s output is weakly decreasing in <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"c.\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span></span><span class=\"mord\">.</span></span></span></span></span> (One can do this very generally, without making any assumptions about the demand curve.)</p></div><div class=\"pq-ans pq-ans--whole\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Shown, with no assumption at all on the demand curve.</p></div></div>"}